.Dd November 22, 2026
.Dt NVRM_CHAN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nvrm_chan_create ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_submit ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_kick ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_gp_space ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_wait ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_status_get ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_info_get ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_alloc_get ,
.Nm nvrm_chan_destroy
.Nd ordered GPFIFO submission and channel progress
.Sh LIBRARY
libnvrm
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In nvrm/nvrm_chan.h
.Vt "typedef struct nvrm_chan nvrm_chan;"
.Vt nvrm_chan_params ;
.Vt nvrm_gp_entry ;
.Vt nvrm_submit_opts ;
.Vt nvrm_submit_result ;
.Vt nvrm_chan_status ;
.Vt nvrm_chan_info ;
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_create "nvrm_client *cl" "const nvrm_chan_params *p" "nvrm_chan **chp"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_submit "nvrm_chan *ch" "const nvrm_gp_entry *e" "unsigned n" "const nvrm_submit_opts *o" "nvrm_submit_result *r"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_kick "nvrm_chan *ch"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_gp_space "nvrm_chan *ch" "unsigned *n"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_wait "nvrm_chan *ch" "uint64_t point" "uint64_t timeout_ns"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_status_get "nvrm_chan *ch" "nvrm_chan_status *st"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_info_get "const nvrm_chan *ch" "nvrm_chan_info *info"
.Ft int
.Fn nvrm_chan_alloc_get "nvrm_client *cl" "nvrm_chan_alloc *out"
.Ft void
.Fn nvrm_chan_destroy "nvrm_chan *ch"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A channel submits pushbuffer segments to a GPU engine through a GPFIFO ring.
The core owns the ring, submission order and progress semaphore; the backend
only publishes the ring pointer, rings the doorbell and reports execution and
errors.
.Ss Creation and information
.Fn nvrm_chan_create
requires a client opened with
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP .
In
.Vt nvrm_chan_params ,
.Va engines
is a nonzero combination of
.Dv NVRM_ENG_3D ,
.Dv NVRM_ENG_COMPUTE ,
.Dv NVRM_ENG_I2M ,
.Dv NVRM_ENG_2D
and
.Dv NVRM_ENG_COPY .
The first four select
.Dv NVRM_RUNLIST_GRAPHICS ;
copy alone selects
.Dv NVRM_RUNLIST_COPY .
A graphics channel may include copy when the backend supports that pairing.
.Va entries
is a power of two from
.Dv NVRM_CHAN_ENTRIES_MIN
through
.Dv NVRM_CHAN_ENTRIES_MAX ;
zero selects
.Dv NVRM_CHAN_ENTRIES_DEFAULT .
.Va flags
must be zero.
On success,
.Fa *chp
receives the channel; on failure it is
.Dv NULL .
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_chan_info_get
fills
.Vt nvrm_chan_info
with the requested engines, ring size and runlist, the GPFIFO class, the class
bound on each of eight subchannels, and
.Va sem_va ,
the GPU address of the 64-bit progress semaphore.
The ring and the host release segments are mapped read-only for the GPU, so a
pushbuffer that writes them faults its channel; the semaphore has a page of its
own, mapped read-write, so the GPU can write nothing else.
Subchannels 0 through 4 are respectively 3D, compute, inline-to-memory, 2D and
copy.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_chan_alloc_get
fills
.Vt nvrm_chan_alloc
with the parameters the RM was given for the last channel allocation this
client submitted, whether the RM took them or refused them.
The field names are the RM's own and the handles are the RM's own numbers, so
a caller can print every field of what was sent.
The parameters do not say which field the RM refused: they are the whole
request, and only the status of the allocation says which request the RM
refused, so a caller that wants to know which field was wrong must compare
the two itself.
A client that submitted no channel allocation to the RM is told so with
.Dv NVRM_E_NOENT ,
and its structure is left as it was; a backend that keeps no such parameters
refuses the call with
.Dv NVRM_E_NOTSUP .
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_chan_status_get
fills
.Vt nvrm_chan_status
with
.Dv NVRM_CHAN_ALIVE
or
.Dv NVRM_CHAN_DEAD ,
the error notifier, GP_GET and GP_PUT, and the submitted and completed progress
points.
.Ss Submission and capacity
Each
.Vt nvrm_gp_entry
names a pushbuffer segment by GPU address
.Va va
and length in
.Va dwords .
Addresses are nonzero, four-byte aligned and below 2^40; lengths are from 1
through 2^21-1.
.Dv NVRM_GP_NO_PREFETCH
requests a fetch only after prior work finishes.
.Dv NVRM_GP_INCOMPLETE
joins an element to the next one.
The final element cannot be incomplete.
.Pp
A ring of E entries holds at most E-1 pending entries because equal GP_GET and
GP_PUT means empty.
Every batch also needs one core entry that releases the progress semaphore, so
a batch carries at most E-2 caller elements.
That core release is the semaphore release the library appends to every batch:
one extra ring entry that writes the progress point of the batch to the
semaphore.
.Fn nvrm_chan_gp_space
reports free caller-element capacity after reserving that release entry.
The GPU may move GP_GET past a ring slot before it has read the release
segment the slot names, so a slot that holds a core release counts as free
only once the progress semaphore has reached that release: a batch never
rewrites a segment whose release has not run.
.Pp
A caller therefore sees less room than the slots GP_GET has passed: a slot
whose core release has not run yet is not free.
A submit with a zero timeout can return
.Er NVRM_E_TIMEOUT
after accepting part of its elements; the accepted count and the point of the
last accepted batch are in the result.
.Fn nvrm_chan_wait
on that point frees the slots those batches held.
.Fn nvrm_chan_submit
splits a larger request into ordered batches.
An incomplete run and its terminator are indivisible and never straddle batches.
If the next unit does not fit, submission waits for GP_GET to advance.
.Vt nvrm_submit_opts
may be
.Dv NULL
for an unlimited wait and an immediate doorbell.
Its
.Va timeout_ns
is zero for no wait or
.Dv UINT64_MAX
for no limit.
.Dv NVRM_SUBMIT_NO_KICK
publishes GP_PUT without ringing the doorbell; a submit that must then wait for
ring space rings the doorbell for the work it has published first, because
that work is what holds the ring.
.Vt nvrm_submit_result
returns the number of caller elements accepted and the last batch's point.
A refused validation writes no ring entries; a timeout can follow earlier
accepted batches, which the result reports.
.Pp
Submitters queue on the channel and are served strictly in arrival order.
A later small submission cannot pass an earlier waiter.
A queued submit still honours its own
.Va timeout_ns :
once its deadline passes while earlier submits are ahead of it, it leaves the
queue without placing anything and the order of the others is unchanged.
When the submit at the head finishes or leaves, the next one is woken at once.
Each batch stores its entries, applies the normal-memory store barrier,
publishes GP_PUT, applies the device barrier, then rings the doorbell unless
.Dv NVRM_SUBMIT_NO_KICK
was given.
.Fn nvrm_chan_kick
applies the device barrier and rings once for published work still awaiting a
doorbell; with none pending it does nothing.
.Ss Progress and failure
.Fn nvrm_chan_wait
waits for the progress semaphore to reach
.Fa point .
Point zero and an already completed point return immediately.
The checks of a wait that is not yet satisfied run in this order: a
.Xr fork 2
child is refused, then a dead channel is lost, and only then is a point above
the submitted count invalid.
A zero
.Fa timeout_ns
does not sleep;
.Dv UINT64_MAX
means no limit.
.Pp
An execution fault kills only its channel.
A dead channel stays dead: submission and kicking fail, while status,
information and destruction remain available.
.Va error
in
.Vt nvrm_chan_status
is the channel's error notifier and belongs to the backend: the fake backend
reports 13 for an unsupported method or class and 31 for a GPU address fault,
and the RM backend reports the RM notifier code.
The core reports one value itself,
.Dv NVRM_CHAN_ERR_SEMAPHORE :
the progress semaphore, which the GPU can write, held a value above the
submitted point or below the completed point, and the channel is dead because
that value cannot be progress.
The message of a call that fails for a dead channel names the notifier and says
to destroy the channel and create a new one.
Waiting for an already completed point still succeeds; waiting for later work
on a dead channel fails.
.Fn nvrm_chan_destroy
releases the backend channel, its maps, memory and VA reservation.
When the backend refuses to destroy its channel, the ring memory and VA stay
held, because the backend may still use them: the refusal is counted in
.Va failed_releases
of
.Xr nvrm_mem 3
and its message kept, and
.Fn nvrm_client_close
makes the backend drop its record of the channel, without touching the
device, before it frees them.
.Fn nvrm_client_close
destroys every channel still live on the client first.
.Pp
Submission, kicking, capacity queries, waits and status queries may run
concurrently on one channel.
Creation and destruction must not run concurrently with any call on the same
client.
In a
.Xr fork 2
child, creation, submission and kicking are refused, and a wait for a point
not yet completed is refused; capacity, status and completed-point waits answer
from the state the child inherited and make no backend call.
Destruction drops only the child's records and makes no backend call.
.Pp
The message of
.Fn nvrm_client_errmsg
belongs to the most recent call on the client.
Every call of this page, including
.Fn nvrm_chan_info_get ,
clears it and writes it under one lock of the client, so concurrent calls do not
race on it; but it is only meaningful when no other thread calls into the same
client meanwhile, because another call replaces it.
.Ss Structure sizes
The first member of every public options, result, status and information
structure is
.Va size .
Zero or the structure's current
.Li sizeof
selects this version; another value is invalid.
.Sh IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
The RM backend makes one channel group (a TSG) per channel, with the engine
of the runlist, the channel itself of the GPFIFO class, and one engine object
per engine the mask asks for.
The userd page is one 4 KiB page of system memory that the GPU reads and the
CPU writes: the GPFIFO control words (the get and the put offsets) are in it.
The error notifier is one more page of system memory, read-only for the CPU.
The usermode page of the client is the only device mapping the backend makes:
the doorbell is one aligned 32-bit store of the work submit token in it.
The page is in the GPU's BAR, so it is device memory and its CPU mapping is
made on the GPU's own device node, not on the control device: the RM maps
device memory through the BAR aperture and refuses a mapping of it named on
the control device.
The copy class is not bound on a graphics runlist yet: a channel that asks
for both is refused.
The class list of the device is read whole: it holds as many entries as the
RM's own class-list control does (200 in this RM version), and a device that
lists more classes than that is refused by name, with both numbers, not cut.
The channel names no VA space: the channel group carries it, and a channel
under a group that names one is refused by the RM with
.Dv NV_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT .
The GP FIFO offset is the GPU address of the channel's ring: the field is
the virtual offset of the GP FIFO, the same kind of value the RM's own
kernel client passes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The calls returning
.Vt int
return
.Dv NVRM_OK
on success or an error code below.
.Fn nvrm_client_errmsg
returns a one-line explanation for the channel's client.
.Fn nvrm_chan_destroy
returns no value and accepts
.Dv NULL .
.Sh EXAMPLES
The live proof of these calls is the channel probe of the test-boot kit:
.Pa tests/libnvrm/chan_probe.c
creates a compute channel, pushes a semaphore release through it and reads the
payload the GPU wrote back, measures the latency of a thousand releases, runs
a stress loop, and shows that a push at an address that is not mapped kills
one channel while another one keeps working.
.Pp
.Pa tests/libnvrm/mem_probe.c
measures the memory calls of the same kit.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ".Fn nvrm_chan_status_get"
.It Fn nvrm_chan_create
returns
.Er NVRM_E_DENIED
for a fork child or a client without
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP ,
.Er NVRM_E_INVAL
for invalid parameters,
.Er NVRM_E_NOTSUP
when channels or the requested graphics/copy pairing are unsupported,
.Er NVRM_E_NOMEM
when allocation fails, and
.Er NVRM_E_NOSLOT ,
.Er NVRM_E_RANGE ,
.Er NVRM_E_RM
or
.Er NVRM_E_IO
when the underlying reservation, allocation or mapping operation reports that
failure.
.It Fn nvrm_chan_submit
returns
.Er NVRM_E_DENIED
in a fork child,
.Er NVRM_E_INVAL
for invalid options, result size or elements (an INCOMPLETE run needs one ring
slot per element and at most E-2 fit in a batch, so a longer run is invalid),
.Er NVRM_E_TIMEOUT
when ring space does not become available by the deadline, or when earlier
submits on the channel still hold the queue at the deadline (nothing is
accepted in that case), and
.Er NVRM_E_LOST
when the channel is dead.
.It Fn nvrm_chan_kick
returns
.Er NVRM_E_DENIED
in a fork child and
.Er NVRM_E_LOST
when the channel is dead.
.It Fn nvrm_chan_gp_space
returns
.Er NVRM_E_LOST
when the channel is dead.
.It Fn nvrm_chan_wait
returns
.Er NVRM_E_DENIED
in a fork child when the point is not yet completed,
.Er NVRM_E_INVAL
when the point exceeds the submitted count,
.Er NVRM_E_TIMEOUT
when the point is not completed by the deadline, and
.Er NVRM_E_LOST
when unfinished work belongs to a dead channel.
.It Fn nvrm_chan_status_get
and
.Fn nvrm_chan_info_get
return
.Er NVRM_E_INVAL
for an unsupported structure size.
.It Fn nvrm_chan_alloc_get
returns
.Er NVRM_E_INVAL
for a null structure or an unsupported structure size,
.Er NVRM_E_NOENT
when the client submitted no channel allocation to the RM, and
.Er NVRM_E_NOTSUP
when the backend records no channel allocation parameters.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
